{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_8e8c0bb9eceec49b","type":"tool_result","content":"147:def _resolve_magpie_python() -> str:\n148-    \"\"\"Resolve the Python interpreter for Magpie subprocesses.\n149-\n150-    Order: $MAGPIE_PYTHON (only when it can ``import Magpie``) > first PATH\n151-    ``python3`` that can ``import Magpie`` > /opt/venv/bin/python when present\n152-    > first PATH ``python3``. A stale ``$MAGPIE_PYTHON`` is validated and\n153-    skipped to avoid ``ModuleNotFoundError`` at benchmark time.\n154-\n155-    Returns:\n156-        str: Path to a Python interpreter that can import Magpie, falling back\n157-        to an existing interpreter that preflight can install Magpie into.\n158-    \"\"\"\n159-\n160-    def _can_import_magpie(py: str) -> bool:\n161-        \"\"\"Whether an interpreter can import Magpie and its ``yaml`` dep.\n162-\n163-        Probes both ``Magpie`` and ``yaml`` so interpreters that resolve\n164-        Magpie via a ``.pth`` but lack PyYAML are rejected.\n165-\n166-        Args:\n167-            py: Path to the candidate Python interpreter.\n168-\n169-        Returns:\n170-            ``True`` if both imports succeed in the interpreter.\n171-        \"\"\"\n172-        try:\n173-            # Probe with ``importlib.util.find_spec`` rather than a bare\n174-            # ``import`` so a missing module returns a non-zero exit code\n175-            # WITHOUT the child emitting a ``ModuleNotFoundError`` traceback.\n176-            # ``run_with_session_kill`` mirrors child stderr to the parent\n177-            # stream, so a bare ``import Magpie`` on a candidate that lacks it\n178-            # would leak an alarming traceback into the run log even though the\n179-            # probe failing is an expected, benign step of interpreter\n180-            # resolution.\n181-            proc = run_with_session_kill(\n182-                [\n183-                    py,\n184-                    \"-c\",\n185-                    \"import importlib.util as u, sys; \"\n186-                    \"sys.exit(0 if u.find_spec('Magpie') and u.find_spec('yaml') else 1)\",\n187-                ],\n--\n249:    2. ``_resolve_magpie_python()`` — the interpreter that runs the benchmark\n250-       harness; on a single-venv install this is also the vLLM venv.\n251-    3. The interpreter behind the ``vllm`` executable (``<venv>/bin/python``\n252-       alongside ``shutil.which(\"vllm\")``) when it exists on disk.\n253:    4. ``_resolve_magpie_python()``'s canonical fallback via step 2.\n254-\n255-    Args:\n256-        framework (str): Framework whose install the probe must inspect.\n257-\n258-    Returns:\n259-        str: Path to the interpreter the probe should invoke.\n260-    \"\"\"\n261-    if (framework or \"\").strip().lower() == \"vllm\":\n262-        venv_root = os.environ.get(\"VLLM_VENV_ROOT\", \"\").strip()\n263-        if venv_root:\n264-            venv_python = str(Path(venv_root) / \"bin\" / \"python\")\n265-            if os.access(venv_python, os.X_OK):\n266-                return venv_python\n267:    magpie_python = _resolve_magpie_python()\n268-    # Prefer the harness interpreter; on a single-venv box it already IS the\n269-    # vLLM venv.\n270-    if magpie_python and magpie_python != \"/opt/venv/bin/python\":\n271-        return magpie_python\n272-    # Fell through to the canonical default; pin the venv that backs ``vllm\n273-    # serve`` so the probe hits the real server source.\n274-    vllm_exe = shutil.which(\"vllm\")\n275-    if vllm_exe:\n276-        vllm_python = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(vllm_exe), \"python\")","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9087e8e0-3c3d-46ce-bdfb-3ddcba42adf6","uuid":"488417a3-5caa-4af7-b16c-bf1673bcec34","timestamp":"2026-09-14T11:52:38.610Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"147:def _resolve_magpie_python() -> str:\n148-    \"\"\"Resolve the Python interpreter for Magpie subprocesses.\n149-\n150-    Order